essly harassed  both by the tarnado and sometimes by  their own neighbors next door  furthermore there is growing evidence  that there has been a sharp increase  in the number of violent events  targeting rohingya muslims  since 2011 which was the year in which  myanmar started as a country a  transition into democracy  the latest outbreak of violence the  infamous military crackdown  that took place in 2017 alone  displays over 700 000 rohingya muslims  the vast majority of whom are currently  living in bangladesh  in crowded refugee camps and one of the  things that one of the facts that we're  going to present to you today  is that very large proportions of this  refugee of the refugees living in cox's  bazar  is that the vast majority of them report  having survived  or witnessed physical assault murder  rape and arson attacks  and despite how pervasive these  narratives are  what happened exactly in the aftermath  of the 2017 crackdown and why it  happened  remains highly contested and our paper  will speak directly to the two  predominant  narratives and explanations for what  happened and why  the first is that of the international  community  which primarily agrees that the kind of  violence  that the rohingya have been exposed to  especially during and after 2017  are a textbook example of ethnic  cleansing  and that the evidence of mass killings  and mass  gun graves that they have been exposed  to show a clear  genocidal intent on the side of the  military  on the other hand the competing  narrative is that of the  former civilian government of myanmar  now the polls  former state counselor ansan suchi  declared that it was still not easy to  establish  clear patterns on it of events and that  it's possible that some refugees might  have  provided inaccurate or exaggerated  information  so the bottom line is the government of  myanmar  argues that there is no evidence that  the persecution of rohingya muslims or  violence against rohingya muslims  has bee